            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

&lt;b&gt;Observed Air Leakage:&lt;/b&gt;

Overall, your house has a lot of excess air leakage, however there are some key areas where air leakage occurs. Air leakage was observed through electrical outlets on your exterior walls, the weather stripping/door sweep along the back door, and along the baseboards (between the floor and wall). As you are already in process of adding additional insulation to the exterior, re-wrapping the home will allow the air tightness to be improved. Electrical plug/switch gaskets should be installed on any exterior wall locations to reduce drafts through these locations. 



Air leakage was also observed through both bathroom exhaust fans. I recommend you replace your exhaust fans with an ENERGY STAR® rated model.



The attic access hatch in the hallway closet I suspect is one the major sources of air leakages in the home, as it is essentially an open hole into the cold attic for air to travel. I recommend finishing the attic access hatch, and ensuring an airtight fit if an access door is designed/planned. 



There was some air leakage observed along the header spacing in the basement. I recommend using spray foam in the voids, which will insulate and reduce air leakage as well. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

For a minimum 10% improvement you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 90&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;3.1 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.  For your home, I believe a 20% to 30% improvement is attainable. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must have a home energy evaluation, which includes an air leakage test, before and after your retrofits. For a full rebate, the rebate application, with a copy of the first page of both home energy evaluation reports, must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of the second energy evaluation. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x4 studs with 4 inches (R12) of fiberglass batt insulation. I have modelled adding 4 inches (R20) of semi-rigid, breathable exterior insulation.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding R20 of semi-rigid, breathable exterior insulation, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 550&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;18.5 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 1,875 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 0.7 (R-3.8). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</mainWalls>
                <ceilings>

The attic of the original home was assumed to be R28, the attic over the second bedroom/bathroom was assumed to be R40, and the attic over the kitchen was assumed to be R14. I have modelled adding insulation to each section, bringing the insulation value of each attic to R50. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By increasing the insulation to R50 in each section, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 230&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;7.7 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 125 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total ceiling area and achieve a minimum insulation value of RSI 7.0 (R-40). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of ceiling area that is insulated compared to the total ceiling area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</ceilings>
                <foundation>

&lt;b&gt;Basement&lt;/b&gt;

Your basement foundation walls appear to be 8 inches of concrete with no insulation. I have modeled adding 2 inches (R10) rigid foam, extruded polystyrene, insulation, as well as 2x4 framing with 4 inches (R12) of fiberglass batt insulation. 



Your basement pony walls appear to be concrete blocks, as well as 2x4 framing with 4 inches (R12) of fiberglass batt insulation. I have modelled adding 2 inches (R10) rigid foam, extruded polystyrene, insulation to the pony walls. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding 2 inches (R10) rigid foam extruded polystyrene insulation and 2x4 framing with 4 inches (R12) of fiberglass batt insulation to the basement walls, and 2 inches (R10) rigid foam, extruded polystyrene, insulation to the pony walls, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 560&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;18.7 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 880 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total foundation wall area including crawlspaces (when applicable), and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.8 (R-10). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



&lt;b&gt;Crawl Space&lt;/b&gt;

Your crawlspace walls appear to be concrete, plus 2x4 studs with 4 inches (R12) of fiberglass batt insulation. I recommend you add 2 inches (R10) rigid foam, extruded polystyrene, insulation to the interior of your crawlspace walls.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding 2 inches (R10) rigid foam, extruded polystyrene, insulation to the crawlspace walls, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 250&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;8.5 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 145 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate 100 percent of the crawl space wall area, including the header area. The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</foundation>
                <VentilationSystem>

During my assessment, there appeared to be minimal ventilation for your home, this is most likely why you noticed moisture/ice buildup along the windows. Proper ventilation protects a home from unwanted toxins, pollutants, and odors and can also help protect the overall structure of a home or building by eliminating excess moisture from the air. An energy-efficient heat recovery ventilator is one of the best ways to control indoor air quality.



I recommend that you have an ENERGY STAR® rated Heat Recovery Ventilator (HRV) system designed and installed by an individual who holds current certification from an organization, such as HRAI. An HRV could be tied into the existing furnace duct work to minimize installation requirements. When choosing and HRV, look for a model that is ENERGY STAR® rated, and also has a recirculation mode. Depending on the design of the system, the recirculation mode could be used to help distribute the warm air throughout the home when using the wood stove in the livingroom. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By installing an ENERGY STAR® rated HRV, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 10&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;0.6 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are currently no rebates available for installing an HRV from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</VentilationSystem>
                <HotWaterSystem>

If you decide to consider propane as a fuel source (see heating option 2 above), I recommend that you replace your electric hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your electric hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 1,140&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 1,250 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

&lt;b&gt;Option 1: Direct Vented Oil-fired Furnace&lt;/b&gt;

Your current oil fired furnace was originally rated at 83% AFUE. When it is time to replace it, I have modeled an upgrade to a Direct Vented oil furnace with a minimum efficiency of 87% AFUE. If you choose a direct vented oil furnace (vented out a side wall), the current chimney can be eliminated altogether.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your heating system with a direct vented oil-fired furnace with an efficiency of 87% AFUE, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 520&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;17.8 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are no rebates available for an oil-fired furnace with an efficiency less than 92% AFUE. 



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



&lt;b&gt;Option 2: Condensing Propane Furnace&lt;/b&gt;

If you choose to consider propane as a fuel source, I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR® qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE. If you choose a high-efficiency condensing propane furnace, the current chimney can be eliminated altogether.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your heating system with a high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 1,040&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;31.8 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Woodstove Insert Contributions&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

I have estimated the savings by using your woodstove insert for 50% of your heating load. The cost of harvesting a cord of wood is estimated to be $ 100 per cord. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By using your wood stove for 50% of your heating demand, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 1,500&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs. 



&lt;b&gt;Note:&lt;/b&gt; There was some air leakage observed coming from around the fireplace insert. As you are repairing and replacing the wall around the wood stove, I would suggest to ensure the unit is sealed well in place to avoid cold drafts/heat loss around this area. 



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Fuel Costs - Yellowknife&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $19.45 min, $0.32 per kWh

Oil  - $1.12 per litre 

Propane - $0.71 per litre

Wood - $341 per cord 

Pellets - $5.67 per bag



